title: Detecting Edges
files:
  - em.prog:em.examples.basic/Ex05_Button1P.em.ts
  - em.prog:em.examples.basic/Ex06_Button2P.em.ts
  - em.core:em.hal/EdgeI.em.ts
  - em.core:em.hal/GpioI.em.ts
  - rpi.2040:rpi.distro.2040/BoardC.em.ts
  - .assets/img-101-09-01.png
  - .assets/img-101-09-02.png

steps:

  - cmds:
      - view 6
    text: |

      {[ht,$start] Edge Detection}

      This tour visits two programs which wink `AppLed` &ndash; but now in response to asynchrnously pressing `AppBut`:

      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} {[cu]Button1P}, which uses a primitive _super-loop_<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} {[cu]Button2P}, which relies upon _fiber dispatch_

      {[in]An earlier <b>One-Shot Timers</b> tour took a similar ap&shy;proach &ndash; contrasting two similar program designs. }

      Looking at the {[cu]Button1P} signal capture, you can press `AppBut` at any point in time and for any duration.

      Here, {[cu]Button1P} winks `AppLed` at the _beginning_ of the button press &ndash; when `AppBut` transitions 1&thinsp;&rarr;&thinsp;0.



  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 7
      - mark 1 B 12
      - mark 1 C 20
      - mark 1 D 28
      - mark 1 E 37
    focus: [1,11]
    acts: [$reveal,$build]
    text: |

      {[ht,$details] %[cu]Button1P% &ndash; Details}

      Like the {[cu]OneShot1P} program seen prior, {[cu]Button1P} main&shy;tains its portability by using an {[cu]AppButEdge} {[cs]$delegate}:

      {[bm,A]} We access `AppBut` via the current {[cu]BoardC} <span class="em codicon codicon-circuit-board"></span>.<br>
      {[bm,B]} We bind a detection handler at build-time [**`meta`**].<br>
      {[bm,C]} We setup the `AppBut` pin HW at run-time [**`targ`**].<br>
      {[bm,D]} We enable `AppBut` detection inside our super-loop.<br>
      {[bm,E]} We clear dection before signaling {[cn]pressed_flag}.

      {[in]The <b>EMS</b> translator ensures all %[cs]em$startup% code exe&shy;cutes <i>before</i> the target C++ program reaches %[cs]em$run%.}


  - cmds:
      - open 2
      - mark 2 A 10
      - mark 2 B 15
      - mark 2 C 26
      - mark 2 D 29
      - mark 2 E 38
    focus: [2,8]
    acts: [$reveal,$build]
    text: |

      {[ht,$details] %[cu]Button2P% &ndash; Details}

      In contrast with {[cu]Button1P}, the {[cu]Button2P} program uses {[cu]FiberMgr} in a conventional manner:

      {[bm,A]} We declare a {[cs]$config} which holds a {[ct]Fiber} object.<br>
      {[bm,B]} We bind a new {[ct]Fiber} and its callback at build-time.<br>
      {[bm,C]} We start dispatching {[ct]Fiber} objects at run-time.<br>
      {[bm,D]} {[cf]blinkFB} winks `AppLed` and re-enables `AppBut`.<br>
      {[bm,E]} {[cf]handler} invokes {[cf]post} to schedule {[cf]blinkFB}.

      {[in]Here again, prefer <i>fiber dispatch</i> over an event <i>super-loop</i> when designing your application code.}

  - cmds:
      - view 7
    text: |

      {[ht,monitor_heart] %[cu]Button2P% &ndash; Signal Capture}

      Using {[cu]Button2P}, we'll now zoom-in on a single `AppLed` wink triggered by the falling edge of `AppBut`.

      For orientation, labels {[bm,C]} and {[bm,D]} mark execution of {[cd]%%c} and {[cd]%%d} inside {[cf]handler} and {[cf]blinkFB}.

      The pulses on `DbgB` track program entry into _lite-sleep_ (after {[cf]wink}) versus _deep-sleep_ (after {[cf]enableDetect}).

      {[in]Unlike timer events, we see the <i>exact</i> point where `AppBut` falls and can precisely measure latency.}


  - cmds:
      - open 5
      - mark 5 A 27
      - mark 5 B 28
      - mark 5 C 60
      - mark 5 D 61
    focus: [5,31]
    acts: [$reveal]
    text: |

      {[ht,$details] %[cu]BoardC% &ndash; Details}

  - cmds:
      - open 3
    focus: [3,1]
    acts: [$reveal]
    text: |

      {[ht,$peek] %[cu]EdgeI% &ndash; Quick Look}

  - cmds:
      - open 4
    focus: [4,1]
    acts: [$reveal]
    text: |

      {[ht,$peek] %[cu]GpioI% &ndash; Quick Look}

  - cmds:
    text: |

      {[ht,$extra] Extra Credit}

  - cmds:
    text: |

      {[ht,$steps] Next Steps}



